{"id":"bfa77785-931a-42f2-8e9a-c6ebc986b8df","article_key":"doi:10.1038/s41380-026-03906-8","title":"Multivariate genome-wide association study of post-traumatic stress disorder, alcohol use, and alcohol use disorders","title_zh":"多变量全基因组关联研究：创伤后应激障碍、酒精使用及酒精使用障碍","doi":"10.1038/s41380-026-03906-8","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41380-026-03906-8","abstract":"Alcohol use disorder (AUD) commonly co-occurs with posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Comorbid PTSD and AUD is associated with poorer outcomes including worse treatment prognosis and significant physical health consequences. Both PTSD and AUD are polygenic in nature and genetically overlap. Previous work showed negative or non-significant associations between PTSD and alcohol consumption but positive genetic associations between PTSD and AUD. This work highlights the need for more nuanced examination of the similarities and distinctions in the associations between PTSD and alcohol consumption versus AUD. We leveraged the latest large-scale GWAS data to perform a multivariate GWAS of alcohol consumption (ALCC), problematic alcohol use (ALCP), and PTSD using GenomicSEM. Partial genetic correlations revealed that ALCP and PTSD were associated with each other (rG = 0.39, p = 4.38 × 10-60) and with other psychiatric problems, medical conditions, and pain, while ALCC was only weakly correlated with PTSD (rG = -0.08, p = 2.03 × 10-4) and uncorrelated with most traits after accounting for its genetic overlap with ALCP and PTSD. We examined associations between GenomicSEM-derived polygenic scores (PGS) and their corresponding phenotypes in participants from the Collaborative Study on the Genetics of Alcoholism (COGA). PGS for ALCC were unrelated to PTSD diagnosis and PGS for PTSD were unrelated to drinks in a typical week. PTSD is more strongly related to alcohol problems, and much of the overlap in PTSD and consumption is accounted for by its overlap with alcohol problems. These results help demonstrate the complex partial overlaps of PTSD, AUD, and alcohol consumption.","abstract_zh":"酒精使用障碍（AUD）常与创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）共病。合并患有PTSD和AUD的情况与预后不良有关，包括治疗预后更差以及显著的身体健康后果。PTSD和AUD均为多基因性状，且存在遗传重叠。先前的研究显示，PTSD与酒精摄入之间存在负相关或非显著关联，但PTSD与AUD之间存在正向遗传关联。本研究强调了需要更细致地审视PTSD与酒精摄入与AUD之间关联的相似性与差异性。我们利用最新的大规模GWAS数据，利用GenomicSEM对酒精摄入（ALCC）、有问题的酒精使用（ALCP）及PTSD进行了多变量GWAS。部分遗传相关性揭示，ALCP与PTSD相互关联（rG = 0.39, p = 4.38 × 10-60），并与其他精神疾病、医学状况及疼痛相关；而ALCC仅与PTSD弱相关（rG = -0.08, p = 2.03 × 10-4），在考虑其与ALCP和PTSD的遗传重叠后，ALCC与大多数性状均不相关。我们利用来自酒精遗传学合作研究（COGA）参与者的数据，检验了基于GenomicSEM的多基因评分（PGS）与其对应性状之间的关联。ALCC的PGS与PTSD诊断无关；PTSD的PGS与每周典型饮酒量无关。PTSD与酒精问题的相关性更强，PTSD与摄入量之间的大部分重叠归因于其与酒精问题的重叠。这些结果有助于展示PTSD、AUD及酒精摄入之间复杂的部分重叠。","snippet":"Alcohol use disorder (AUD) commonly co-occurs with posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Comorbid PTSD and AUD is associated with poorer outcomes including worse treatment prognosis and significant physical health consequences. Both PTSD and AUD are polygenic in nature and genetically overlap. Previous work showed negative or non-significant associations between PTSD and alcohol consumption but positive genetic asso…","translated_at":"2026-11-10T03:19:17.154541+00:00","translation_model":"nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning-30b-a3b","source_category":"doi","article_type":"journal-article","volume":null,"issue":null,"published_at":"2026-10-28T00:00:00","acquired_at":"2026-10-29T21:24:47Z","display_date":"2026-10-29T21:24:47Z","display_date_source":"acquired","online_published_at":"2026-10-28T00:00:00","print_published_at":null,"first_author":"Peter B. Barr","authors_text":"Peter B. Barr, Kaitlin Bountress, Chris Chatzinakos, James E. Hart, Zoe E. Neale, Christina Sheerin, Emma Johnson, Elizabeth G. Atkinson, Caroline M. Nievergelt, Adam X. Maihofer, Abigail Powers, Bernice Porjesz, Arpana Agrawal, Howard J. Edenberg, Joel Gelernter, Karestan C. Koenen, on behalf of the PTSD workgroup of the Psychiatric Genomic Consortium (PGC-PTSD), Caroline M. Nievergelt, Adam X. Maihofer, Elizabeth G. Atkinson, Chia-Yen Chen,